.he "open (2)                     10/20/26                    open (2)"
.fo "				    -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
open
- open a file for reading or writing


.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
.bd
open(name, flags, mode)
.br
.bd
char *name;
.br
.bd
int flags, mode;


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
The named file is opened for reading (O_RDONLY), writing
(O_WRONLY), or both (O_RDWR).  The flags come from
.bd
<fcntl.h>.
.bd
O_APPEND
makes every write start at the end of the file.
.bd
O_CREAT
creates the file if it does not exist, and then the third
argument is the permission mode, as for
.bd
creat.
It is read only when
.bd
O_CREAT
is set.
.bd
O_EXCL
with
.bd
O_CREAT
fails if the file already exists.
.bd
O_TRUNC
empties an existing file when it is opened for writing.

Only the access mode and
.bd
O_APPEND
reach the system call.  The kernel masks the mode to its low
two bits and keeps
.bd
O_APPEND
in the open file, so the flag is shared by descriptors made
with
.bd
dup
and by
.bd
fork,
and every write repositions to the end of the file as it
stands at that moment.  A
.bd
seek
on such a descriptor is allowed; the next write ignores it.

The rest is built in the library around the system call.
.bd
O_CREAT
opens the name first and, if the error is ENOENT, calls
.bd
creat
with the third argument as the mode.
.bd
creat
opens write-only, so a caller that asked for
.bd
O_RDWR
or
.bd
O_APPEND
has that descriptor closed and the name opened again.  If
another process takes the name between the two calls, the
second open returns that file and the creation mode is not
applied.
.bd
O_EXCL
is a check after the fact: if the first open succeeds, the
file already existed, the descriptor is closed, and the call
fails with EEXIST.
.bd
O_TRUNC
is
.bd
ftruncate
of the descriptor to zero once it is open, and is skipped
for O_RDONLY.

The returned file descriptor should be saved
for subsequent calls to
.bd
read,
.bd
write,
and
.bd
close.

There is a limit of 16 open files per process.
.nf


.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.fi
A non-negative return indicates success, a -1 indicates failure.
On success, a file descriptor (small non-negative number)
is returned.


.in -5
.bd
ASSEMBLER
.in +5
.br
.bd
(open = 5)
.br
